  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222207/assets/11b00ddb6eb89e6acf79a8d5dc32e07a90850b97d8802df293e1240f6cfe444a.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222207/assets/cd63145c6f0513fe78da148b07064eb725c2de88ded4993dabccc0da173d6583.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}}=\\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}.\n$$",
